y hold **Ace of Clubs** and **2 of Spades**.  \n- The board shows: **2♦, 10♦, Q♠, J♥, K♥**.  \n- This gives you **a pair of 2s** (one in hand, one on board).  \n- More importantly, the board contains **Q♠, J♥, K♥** — which are three consecutive high cards. With your **A♣**, you complete the sequence **A, K, Q, J, 10** — but only if they form a straight. Let's verify:  \n  - A, K, Q, J, 10 = **Broadway Straight** (highest possible straight, not a flush since suits are mixed).  \n  - The 10♦, J♥, Q♠, K♥, A♣ — yes! These five cards form a **Straight (10 through A)**.  \n- So, your best 5-card hand is the **Straight: A-K-Q-J-10**, using your Ace and the four community cards.  \n- Your opponent has shown no aggression so far (all checks/calls), suggesting weakness or a cautious draw.  \n- Since the board has two hearts and a diamond, potential flush draws exist, but you block none directly. However, unless the opponent holds two hearts (e.g., 9♥ 8♥), they don’t have a flush.  \n- Given that both players checked through all previous rounds, it’s likely the opponent missed the board or has a weaker hand like middle pair or just high card.  \n- Now, on the River (final betting round), you have the **nuts (straight)** — the highest possible non-flush hand here. There is no full house or four-of-a-kind possible on this board. Only a higher straight (impossible) or flush could beat you — but no straight beats yours (it's the highest), and a flush would require two suited hole cards in opponent’s hand.  \n\nThus, your **Straight (A–K–Q–J–10)** is very strong, likely the winning hand.\n\nNow considering actions:\n- **Fold (-1)**: Clearly suboptimal — you have a strong made hand.\n- **Check (0)**: Safe, may induce a bluff from opponent or let them catch up cheaply. But with showdown value, checking might miss value.\n- **Raise (≥100)**: Since opponent has been passive, a small raise (e.g., 100) could extract value from weaker pairs (like Qx, Jx, or Kx) or draws that missed.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill. You have ~19,900 chips. Going all-in risks scaring away worse hands and only getting called by better (which is rare, since few have flushes). Unlikely to be profitable here.\n\nOptimal play: **Bet small for value**. A minimal raise (100) is within allowed options and serves to extract value without folding out all worse hands.\n\nReason:  \nYou have the **nut straight**, a very strong hand that beats all one-pair holdings and missed draws. Opponent has shown no strength. A small bet maximizes chance of being called while still gaining value. The minimum raise is 100, which is appropriate given stack sizes and prior action.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
