\n\nYour current hand:  \n- You have **no pair** in your hand, but there’s a **pair of Kings** on the board.  \n- You do not have trips or two pair.  \n- You have **4♥ and J♠**, with two hearts on the board (**3♥ K♥**), so you have **four to a flush** (hearts) — specifically, you\'re one heart away from a flush.  \n- You also have an **open-ended straight draw possibility?** Let\'s check:  \n  - You have **J**, board has **K, 3, 4** — wait: you have **4**, board has **3, K** → that gives you **3-4-J-K**, missing a **Q** or **2** for a straight.  \n  - A **Q** would give you **J-Q-K-A** – no A.  \n  - To complete a 5-high straight (A-2-3-4-5)? No.  \n  - The only possible straight is **Q-J-10-9-8**? Not relevant.  \n  - Actually, with **3, 4, J, K**, you’d need a **Q** to make **Q-J-10-9-8**? No — you don’t have 10 or 9.  \n  - Wait: **3, 4, J, K** — the only straight possibility is **A-2-3-4-5** (wheel), but you don’t have 2 or 5.  \n  - So **no straight draw**.  \n- However, you **do have four hearts**: 4♥, 3♥, K♥ → that’s three hearts. You have **one heart in hand**, two on board → total **three hearts**. So you have **a flush draw** (needing one more heart).  \n  - There are **two hearts in your hand?** No — only **4♥** is a heart; **J♠** is spade.  \n  - Board: **3♥, K♥** → that’s two hearts. Your **4♥** makes **three hearts** — so yes, you have **four cards to a flush** (hearts), needing one more heart on turn or river.  \n  - That’s a **flush draw** — about **9 outs** (13 hearts total - 3 seen = 10 left? Wait: you’ve seen 3♥, K♥, 4♥ — that’s 3 hearts. So 10 left? But only 52 cards, 5 known (your 2 + 3 board), so 47 unknown. 13 - 3 = 10 hearts left. But you only need one more. So ~19% chance on turn, ~35% by river. But you’re not there yet.\n\nBut crucially: **you currently have only Jack high**, with a **pair of Kings** on the board. Your opponent could easily have:\n- K-x (better kicker than your J)\n- A-K (nut kicker)\n- K-K (set)\n- Or even A-A, Q-Q, etc.\n\nYou have **no pair**, just **a flush draw** and **Jack high**. So your current made hand is **King-high**, same as board, with **Jack kicker** — very weak.\n\nHowever, you have **potential** via the **flush draw** (9 outs). So folding now throws away equity (~35% chance to hit flush by river).\n\nOpponent raised by **288** on the flop — indicating strength. Likely has at least top pair with good kicker, two pair, or better.\n\nYour options:\n- **Fold (-1)**: Safe, but forfeits equity in the pot.\n- **Call (0)**: Only valid if raise was already called — but opponent raised by 288, so to call you must match that bet. But "0" is listed as valid — which implies **checking behind is allowed**, meaning **no additional bet required**? That contradicts the raise.\n\nWait: this seems inconsistent.\n\nLet’s re-read:\n\n> Your valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 576 (raise the bet), or any value above 576 but lower than your current chips.]\n\nBut opponent **raised by 288**. If it’s your turn after that, then to stay in the hand, you must **call 288**, not check.\n\nBut “0” is listed as **Check**, not Call. That implies the **betting is unopened** — but opponent *did* raise.\n\nThis suggests a **game state inconsistency** — unless “0” here means **call the current bet**.\n\nBut the note says: “0 for Call/Check”\n\nAh! Clarified: **0 means either Call or Check**, depending on context.\n\nSince opponent raised by 288, **0 here means Call 288**.\n\nSo:\n- **0** = Call 288\n- **576** = Raise to 576 total? Or raise by 576?\n\nThe rule says: “>0 for the amount you raise the bet”\n\nAnd “raise must be at least twice the current maximum bet of this round”\n\nCurrent bet is 288 (opponent raised to 288 total? Or by 288?)\n\nFrom history: “your opponent raised bet by 288” → so **added 288 to the pot**, likely making total bet 288 this round.\n\nSo to call: 288\n\nMinimum raise: must be **at least twice the current bet** → so **at least 576**\n\nHence, valid raises start at **576**\n\nSo:\n- **-1**: Fold\n- **-2**: All-in (you have 19,900 → all-in is 19,900)\n- **0**: Call 288\n- **≥576**: Raise (min 576)\n\nNow, should you call?\n\nYou have:\n- Flush draw (9 outs) → ~35% equity by river\n- Currently behind likely hands (K-x, A-K, sets)\n- But you can win if you hit flush\n\nCalling 288 to win what?\n\nWe don’t know pot size, but assume blinds are small. From starting chips (20k), and prior action minimal (previous rounds had small bets), likely pot is around 500–1000.\n\nCall 288 to win, say, 800–1500 → pot odds ~ 1:3 → you need ~25% equity.\n\nYour flush draw gives ~35% chance by river → **positive expected value to call**\n\nRaising? Bluff-raising with a draw?\n\nPossible, but risky. Opponent showed strength. Raising to 576 (adding 576, so total bet 576) would be a **large raise**, likely inducing fold from weaker hands — but strong hands will call or re-raise.\n\nBut you have little fold equity — opponent may have strong hand.\n\nGoing all-in? 19,900 — overkill, likely folds out worse, called by better.\n\nBest play: **Call and see turn** — preserve chips, realize equity.\n\nYou\'re in the lead overall (19,900 vs opponent likely less), so **avoid unnecessary risk**.\n\nBut since EV is positive and cost is low, **calling is correct**.\n\nFolding wastes equity.\n\nRaising is speculative, high risk.\n\n**Call (action 0)** is best.\n\nEvaluation: I have a flush draw with ~9 outs (~35% to hit by river) and currently hold only King-high with Jack kicker. The opponent raised, indicating strength, but the call cost (288) is low relative to pot and my stack. Given the positive expected value of seeing the turn and potential to hit a winning flush, calling is favorable. Raising or going all-in is overly aggressive and risky given my marginal hand. Folding forfeits too much equity.\n\nReason: Calling realizes sufficient equity from my flush draw at favorable pot odds, while minimizing risk in a situation where I am likely behind but drawing to a strong hand.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
