s is the **Turn** round).  \n- Possible hands:  \n  - You have **one pair of Kings** on the board; your Jack and 4 don’t improve it. Your opponent may have a better kicker or even two pair/full house.\n  - There are **two hearts** on board (3♥, K♥), and you have 4♥ — so a **flush draw** is possible if another heart comes on the river.\n  - A straight draw is weak: you’d need a 2 and 5 for a low straight, but only 5 is missing — not likely.\n  - The board has **A♣, K♣, K♥, 3♥** — danger cards for overpairs or two pair. Opponent could have AK, KK, AA, or even A-K for two pair.\n  - Your current hand strength is **weaker than top pair with good kicker** — you’re essentially playing **King-high with a weak kicker (J)**, same pair as board but poor support.\n\n- Checking is safe to see the river without investing more chips.\n- Raising would represent strength (like having AA, AK, or KJ), but your hand doesn't justify bluffing here unless you believe opponent is weak.\n- However, opponent raised by 288 in Round 1 — indicating strength. They might have a strong hand like AK, KK, AA, or even A-K two pair.\n- Going all-in is too risky with such a weak hand.\n- Folding now avoids further loss, but checking keeps you in cheaply to see if you hit a lucky heart (for flush) or if opponent checks back.\n\nReason:  \nGiven the board texture and your weak holding (bottom pair at best, no strong draws), **checking** is the safest and most logical play. It avoids putting more chips in with a weak hand while still allowing you to see the river card. If a heart comes, you might win with a flush. Given that you're ahead in chips overall and in the lead, preserving stack is valuable. No strong evidence suggests your hand is ahead, so passive play is optimal.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
